#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM5020", unit: "U1")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U1.1 Group axioms",[Show that the even integers form a group under addition, and explain why they do not form a group under multiplication.],[List the identity and inverse for addition.],[The multiplicative identity would have to be one.],[Sums and negatives of even integers are even; addition is associative and has identity zero. Thus they form an additive group. A multiplicative identity inside this set would satisfy $e dot 2=2$, forcing $e=1$, which is not even. Hence multiplication does not give a group.],[Check closure, associativity, identity, and inverse; a single failed multiplicative axiom suffices.]),
 p(2,"E","U1.1 Cancellation",[Solve $a x b=c$ in an arbitrary group and prove uniqueness.],[Undo the left factor on the left.],[Multiply first by $a^(-1)$ on the left and then by $b^(-1)$ on the right.],[The solution is $a^(-1)c b^(-1)$. Substitution gives $a(a^(-1)c b^(-1))b=c$. Any solution must have this value after the indicated multiplications, proving uniqueness.],[Keep the factor order; do not commute arbitrary elements.]),
 p(3,"E","U1.2 Element orders",[Find the order and generated subgroup of $[9]$ in $ZZ slash 15 ZZ$.],[List successive multiples or use the gcd formula.],[The order is $15/gcd(15,9)$.],[The order is five. The successive multiples are $[0],[9],[3],[12],[6]$, then $[0]$. These five elements form the generated subgroup.],[Give both the order and the actual residue classes.]),
 p(4,"E","U1.2 Subgroup test",[Prove that the intersection of two subgroups of a group is a subgroup.],[The intersection contains the identity.],[Apply the one-step subgroup test in both subgroups.],[Let $H,K<=G$. Their intersection contains $e$. For $a,b in H inter K$, both subgroups contain $a b^(-1)$, so it lies in the intersection. The subgroup test proves the claim.],[Explain nonemptiness as well as closure under $a b^(-1)$.]),
 p(5,"C","U1.1 Structural identities",[Prove that a group in which $x^2=e$ for every $x$ is abelian.],[Every element is its own inverse.],[Compare $(a b)^(-1)$ with $b^(-1)a^(-1)$.],[For any $a,b$, the hypothesis gives $a^(-1)=a$, $b^(-1)=b$, and $(a b)^(-1)=a b$. The inverse-of-product identity gives $a b=b a$. Thus the group is abelian.],[Apply the hypothesis to the product as well as the individual factors.]),
 p(6,"C","U1.2 Cyclic subgroups",[List all subgroups of $ZZ slash 18 ZZ$ and their orders.],[Subgroups of a cyclic group correspond to divisors of its order.],[For each divisor $d$ of eighteen, use $chevron.l[d]chevron.r$.],[The subgroups are generated by $[1],[2],[3],[6],[9],[18]$, with orders $18,9,6,3,2,1$ respectively. Every subgroup is cyclic; choosing its least positive representative shows its generator divides eighteen, so the list is exhaustive.],[Justify completeness, not only the six constructions.]),
 p(7,"C","U1.2 Generator arithmetic",[Let $a$ have order $n$. Prove $chevron.l a^r chevron.r=chevron.l a^(gcd(n,r)) chevron.r$.],[Use a Bézout identity for $n,r$.],[If $d=u n+v r$, then $a^d=(a^r)^v$.],[Write $d=gcd(n,r)$. Since $d$ divides $r$, $a^r$ is a power of $a^d$, giving one inclusion. Bézout gives $d=u n+v r$, so $a^d=a^(u n)(a^r)^v=(a^r)^v$, giving the reverse inclusion.],[Prove both inclusions and use $a^n=e$.]),
 p(8,"C","U1.2 Subgroup structure",[Prove that the union of two subgroups $H,K$ is a subgroup exactly when one contains the other.],[Assume neither contains the other and choose one element from each difference.],[For $h in H without K$ and $k in K without H$, test $h k$.],[If one subgroup contains the other, their union is that subgroup. Otherwise choose $h in H without K$ and $k in K without H$. If $h k in H$, then $k=h^(-1)(h k) in H$, a contradiction. If $h k in K$, then $h=(h k)k^(-1) in K$, also a contradiction. Thus the union is not closed.],[Use the correct multiplication side in each contradiction.]),
 p(9,"C","U1.1 Noncommutative examples",[Let $r$ rotate a square by a quarter turn and $s$ reflect it in a vertical axis. Show $s r s=r^(-1)$ and use this to prove the symmetry group is nonabelian.],[Follow a vertex through the three motions.],[Reflection reverses orientation, hence reverses the direction of rotation.],[Label vertices cyclically by residues modulo four so that $r(i)=i+1$ and write the reflection as $s(i)=c-i$ modulo four for a fixed residue $c$. Then $s r s(i)=c-(c-i+1)=i-1=r^(-1)(i)$. If $r,s$ commuted, this would give $r=r^(-1)$, contradicting the order four of $r$.],[Give a precise labeling or coordinate calculation.]),
 p(10,"A","U1.2 Automorphisms",[Classify all automorphisms of the additive group $ZZ slash n ZZ$.],[A homomorphism is determined by the image of $[1]$.],[The image must generate the group.],[Every endomorphism is $f_k([r])=[k r]$, since additivity determines all values from $[1]$. This is well defined modulo $n$. Its image is the subgroup generated by $[k]$, of order $n/gcd(n,k)$. Thus it is bijective exactly when $gcd(n,k)=1$. The automorphisms are precisely these multiplication maps; composition corresponds to multiplication of unit residues modulo $n$.],[Prove that every map is accounted for and determine the exact invertibility condition.]),
 p(11,"A","U1.2 Products of cyclic groups",[Prove that $C_m times C_n$ is cyclic exactly when $gcd(m,n)=1$.],[Find the order of a pair of elements.],[A pair of generators has order $"lcm"(m,n)$.],[For any pair $(x,y)$, its order divides $"lcm"(m,n)$ because that exponent kills both coordinates. If the gcd exceeds one, this least common multiple is smaller than $m n$, so no element generates the product. If the gcd is one, a pair of generators has order exactly $"lcm"(m,n)=m n$: an exponent killing both is divisible by both $m,n$. Thus this pair generates.],[Prove impossibility in the noncoprime case for every pair, not just one candidate.]),
 p(12,"A","U1.1 Axioms and finiteness",[Let $M$ be a finite nonempty set with an associative operation and a two-sided identity. Assume left and right cancellation. Prove that $M$ is a group.],[Multiplication by a fixed element is injective.],[On a finite set it is surjective, so left and right inverses exist.],[For $a in M$, both maps $x |-> a x$ and $x |-> x a$ are injective by cancellation and hence surjective. Choose $b,c$ with $a b=e$ and $c a=e$. Associativity gives $c=c(a b)=(c a)b=b$, so $b$ is a two-sided inverse. Every element has an inverse and the other group axioms were assumed.],[Identify precisely where finiteness and associativity enter.]),
)
